a couple of you guys have recently been  asking what the best way to select an  architecture school is  well it's a very difficult question but  today we're going to tackle it head on  [Music]  what's going on guys my name is dave  thomas and welcome back to the channel  if you're new here firstly thank you so  much for joining me on this channel we  talk about  architecture and technology my  background is obviously in architecture  i'm a redstone architect in the state of  western australia  but i'm also a technology lover and an  entrepreneur so if any of those things  interest you make sure you smash that  subscribe button  because today we're talking about  architecture and selecting the perfect  architectural university  there's going to be a lot of different  points in this video so i'm going to try  and make this interesting and creative  by running around this house like a  madman and taking as many shots as i can  architecture can be a pretty demanding  and time consuming course  so the first thing you want to look at  is how long the course  actually is most courses here in  australia are three years  plus two years for your masters plus  another two years in the actual real  world  so if you're looking for a good course  make sure it's about that five year mark  make sure that the course you do select  has pretty good fees and fee  options the last thing you want to be  doing is opening up your fridge and  having absolutely no food in it  whatsoever  when we talk about fees there's only  really two different structures you  should really focus on  one do they offer student loans or two  do they not  some universities give you a student  loan that is at a pretty good rate  for as long as you need so once you  start earning a decent amount of money  you can actually start paying this loan  off other universities don't have this  option which means you have to come up  with the money  straight away before you even start  studying so  for example one university might be ten  thousand dollars a year the other might  be four  there's probably a pretty good reason  for the difference in price but at the  end of the day  you can only go to what you can afford  now another thing you have to consider  is that course online or is it at a  campus  does this matter to you do you want to  be studying from home 24 7 or  do you want to have that experience and  go to a campus  right now i don't know if there's too  much of a difference between courses  if you pick a really good online  university versus a really good campus  university  but it is a personal choice so it's  something you have to consider  personally i think campus is the way to  go because  you get a lot more one-on-one time with  your tutors you really do need  to sit down draw and walk through some  plans  with your tutors if you're going to  study architecture studying online is  all well and good and technology is  definitely caught up to make this  possible  but i still think campus is a much  better option the next thing you have to  consider is how demanding is this course  how many hours do you have to spend at  university or online  is this going to leave you with enough  time to work part-time job  do you need a work part-time job if so  do you even have to step back down and  only do your course part-time  this is just something that you have to  think about before you even begin  thinking about individual universities  or colleges  okay so we've got through the boring  stuff before you even select the  university  now let's say you've narrowed it down to  a few universities in your area  or the ones that you really want to go  to the first thing you want to focus on  is the actual course curriculum do they  have any electives in the first couple  of years  what are those electives those electives  are the ones that are going to  differentiate you from all the other  students in that course  so make sure that you pick an elective  that has some sort of relevance to the  architecture industry  in my personal experience i picked a  graphic design course it taught me  photoshop  indesign illustrator all of those things  and it was fantastic  it was something that i still use to  this day in my professional career other  electives on course are things like  interior design courses  construction management or anything with  technical drawing  you really want to get good at these  elements because you're going to get  tested on them especially construction  management and project management  later on down the track when you go to  register to become an architect  when you're looking through that course  list you want to make sure it's filled  with practical and technical work  and less theory work that's just my  opinion  based on my experience practical work is  things where you actually go out and  design  architecture you design houses you  design commercial properties  and technical work is where you get to  the nitty-gritty details you understand  the formation the construction process  and everything that goes into building a  house the theory is obviously the  history of architecture  and all sorts of different theoretical  architectural principles  this is all well and good and you  definitely have to learn something to  get your head around architecture  but i do believe that your time is  better spent on the practical and the  technical  now you've worked through the curriculum  and you know exactly what you're getting  taught  but who are you getting taught by the  ratio of teachers to students isn't that  important  unless it's one to one hundred for  example if it's one to ten  one to twenty that's pretty normal and  that's okay i personally believe it's  more important that you understand  who's teaching you and who the lecturers  are so for example are the lecturers  only lecturers and have they only  lectured their whole life  or are they actual practicing architects  as well as lecturers  the guys and girls that are practicing  architects as well as lecturers are the  ones that you genuinely want to learn  from  now don't get me wrong here the ones  that have been lecturing for a very long  time are exceptional as well  but i'm thinking more long term here  unless you want to end up as a  researcher in the architecture field  then disregard this advice but if you  want to work as a practicing architect  then make sure you befriend those  architects that are lecturers as well  they're the ones that are most likely  going to give you a job after you've  finished your master's degree  or even potentially during if you're a  high enough achiever so as you walk into  university make sure you have a plan  make sure you know what kind of  architecture you want to do do you want  to be a residential a commercial  architect do you want to work for a big  firm or a small firm  plan this out because you're going to  have to take advantage of that from day  one  you're going to find those lecturers  that are architects and our lecturers at  the same time  hang on to them learn from them and  master as much as you can  because they are more valuable than the  entire course itself  and finally you want to think about the  facilities in the campus does the campus  have 3d printers does it have laser  cutters  cnc machines plotters a1 printers a3  printers  does it have any of this on site and on  campus what about a workshop if you want  to create a model or anything of that  sort if you are studying on campus  these things are usually somewhere the  campuses are usually pretty large  and it takes a little bit of hunting but  make sure you know if they have this at  your university of choice  now a couple of the less important  things in my personal opinion at least  one parking and travel a lot of  universities are cutting down on the  parking and  limitations to traveling to the  university they're encouraging public  transport and riding your bike and all  sorts of sustainable practices  which is great if you're studying  architecture because it all ties in but  if you live pretty far away then you  want to be able to drive and make sure  you can get there on time especially if  you have to go to a part-time  job after so that's just something you  have to take into consideration  if you are going to drive to campus  you're probably going to spend a long  time there as an architect or an  architecture student  i know i spend 48 50 hours there in any  given time without any sleep so campus  amenities are pretty important  does that campus have a nice pub or a  bistro or something you can go to  during your lunch break with your mates  just to hang out and discuss  and just create those real friendships  that are going to last forever  because university is just as important  for learning and knowledge as it is for  creating lifelong friendships you can't  forget about the sporting facilities as  well  does it have a gym does it have a pool  does it have all sorts of things like  this  because again most likely you're going  to be spending most of your time on  campus  so if you can condense your whole life  to that campus it is the most ideal  situation  anyway that's all for me today guys  thank you so much for joining me i hope  you enjoyed this video and i hope you  enjoyed the little quirky house tour at  the same time  if there's anything else you'd like to  know make sure you leave a comment down  below i'm happy to answer  any questions you have whatsoever  because this video forms one of my 28  videos in 28 days for february of 2021  it would usually be i'll see you next  monday but in this case  i'll see you tomorrow

